      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we need to find the inverse of the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\). The projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) onto the vector \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe inverse of this matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) can be found using the formula for the inverse of a 2x2 matrix. For a matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\), the inverse is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nApplying this to our matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{13 \\cdot \\frac{4}{13} - \\frac{6}{13} \\cdot \\frac{6}{13}} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{13} & -\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{9}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n\\[\n13 \\cdot \\frac{4}{13} - \\frac{6}{13} \\cdot \\frac{6}{13} = 4 - \\frac{36}{169} = \\frac{676}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = \\frac{640}{169}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{640}{169}} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{13} & -\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{9}{13} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{169}{640} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{13} & -\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{9}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nMultiply each element of the matrix:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{169}{640} \\cdot \\frac{4}{13} & \\frac{169}{640} \\cdot -\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{169}{640} \\cdot -\\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{169}{640} \\cdot \\frac{9}{13} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{676}{8320} & -\\frac{1014}{8320} \\\\ -\\frac{1014}{8320} & \\frac{1521}{8320} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{169}{2080} & -\\frac{253}{2080} \\\\ -\\frac{253}{2080} & \\frac{1521}{8320} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSince the problem asks for the inverse to exist, the final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{169}{2080} & -\\frac{253}{2080} \\\\ -\\frac{253}{2080} & \\frac{1521}{8320} \\end{pmatrix}}",
