    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the inverse of a projection matrix, we first need to understand the nature of the projection matrix itself. For a vector with two linearly independent components, the projection matrix is represented as a quadratic matrix with only positive elements. In this case, the projection matrix onto the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$ is not invertible, and its inverse does not exist.\n\nThis means $\\mathbf{P}^{-1}$ does not exist, and therefore the zero matrix is the correct answer for $\\mathbf{P}^{-1}$. The zero matrix is a matrix with all elements equal to zero. To represent it, we can write:\n\n$\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ or simply\n\n$\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}$.",
