    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the matrix of projection $\\mathbf{P}$ that projects any vector onto the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, we will use the equation below:\n\n$\\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$. We have the following equation:\n\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThis simplifies to:\n$$-3x - 2y = x.$$\n\nAdding $3x$ to both sides gives: $$\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}},$$\nor $$\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nHowever, the matrix $\\mathbf{P}$ does not have an inverse, as its determinant is zero. This tells us that the matrix of projection onto $\\mathbf{v}$ is not invertible. Therefore, $ \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$ does not exist, and we'll enter the zero matrix.\n\nThus, the final answer is: $\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}$.",
